 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the total amount of\nbonds the Buffalo sewer authority may issue",
    "basePrintNo": "S7837",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7837-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1187, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7837\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the total\namount of bonds the Buffalo sewer authority may issue\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo enable the Buffalo Sewer Authority to issue bonds in the total amount\nof Two Hundred Fifty Million Dollars.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Section 1187(1) of the Public Authorities Law to\nauthorize the Buffalo Sewer Authority to issue bonds in the total amount\nof Two Hundred Fifty Million Dollars from its current authorization of\nOne Hundred Twenty-Five Million Dollars.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date immediately after it shall have become\nlaw.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Buffalo Sewer Authority, is a body corporate and politic constitut-\ning a public benefit corporation created by Chapter 349 of the Laws of\n1935 (now Title 8 of Article 5; Chapter 870 of the Laws of New York of\n1939, the same being Public Authorities Law, Sections 1175 to 1195,\ninclusive).\n\nThe Authority provides sewage and storm water collection, treatment and\ndisposal services for the City of Buffalo and neighboring communities.\nIt has approximately 850 miles of sewers and owns and operates the major\npublicly owned treatment works (\"POTW\") in western New York with a major\nindustrial waste pretreatment program, and has a treatment plant capaci-\nty of 560 million gallons per day (\"MGD\").  All activities and functions\nperformed by the Authority are its direct responsibility.\n\nThe Authority's powers include fixing and collecting sewer rents and\nrates, borrowing money and issuing negotiable bonds, acquiring, holding\nand disposing of personal property for its corporate purposes. The\nAuthority's current bond authority of One Hundred Twenty-Five Million\nDollars ($125 Million) has been in effect since 1975, when it was\nincreased from Seventy-Five Million Dollars. As detailed below, the\nAuthority is now subject to a mandate by the Federal and State govern-\nments that will require the Authority to significantly increase capital\nexpenditures and its issuance of bonds to finance such expenditures.\n\nThe Authority currently has an outstanding bond indebtedness of approxi-\nmately $46,800,000. In 2014, the Authority received approval of its\nCombined Sewer Overflow (\"CSO\") Long Term Control Plan from the United\nStates Environmental Protection Agency (\"EPA\") and the New York State\nDepartment of Environmental Conservation (\"DEC\"), and is subject to an\nEPA Administrative Order to implement the CSO Long Term Control Plan\nover approximately twenty years. This CSO Long Term Control Plan is\nsystem wide, and assesses and prioritizes capital improvement projects\nnecessary to comply with the Administrative Order and the Clean Water\nAct. The total cost of implementing the CSO Long Term Control Plan is\nestimated in 2012 dollars at $380 Million Dollars, and compliance with\nthe EPA Administrative Order will require the Authority to finance the\nCSO Long Term Control Plan capital projects with its own resources,\nincluding bonds, notes, loans and sewer rents and other charges\ncollected from its system users. The Authority estimates that it will\nneed to issue bonds in an aggregate amount of approximately $220 Million\nDollars over the next twenty years, significantly in excess of its\ncurrent bonding authority. Currently, the Authority continues to plan\nand complete projects as identified in the CSO Long Term Control Plan,\nas well as in its five year capital budget. The capital plan expendi-\ntures for 2018-2019 total approximately $38,955,000. Of this amount,\n$16,830,000 is for the Authority's treatment facility rehabilitation and\nimprovement projects, and $22,125,000 is estimated for installation of\ngreen infrastructure and collection system optimization. This increase\nto the Authority's debt limit will help the Authority to avoid a series\nof projected double-digit rate increases expected to begin in fiscal\nyear 2019.  The Authority continues to maintain sufficient operating\nfunds for debt service coverage. As the median household income in the\nCity of Buffalo is $33,119, with more than 30% of the residents living\nin poverty, planned double-digit rate increases will present an undue\nburden for the customer base.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe total amount of the Authority's bonds issued and outstanding is in\nthe aggregate amount of approximately $46,800,000. In addition, absent\nany refunding, the Authority projects an estimated aggregate debt\nservice for its current capital improvement plan of approximately\n$38,955,000, and a total debt service of approximately $85,755,000. As\nnoted, to fully implement the CSO Long Term Control Plan, the Authori-\nty's total debt service will need to increase significantly over the\nnext twenty years, and into the foreseeable future.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
